WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.130
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:02.130
Today we're going to be doing something a
little bit different.

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:06.250
I'm gonna be holding a debate here in my house
around the topic of net neutrality, featuring

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:11.460
me, an internet user and me, representing
a cable company, on opposite sides of the

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.460
table, literally.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.690
Internet User Hank: First things first, for
the viewers at home I think we should just

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.200
define what net neutrality is.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.630
Cable Company Hank: Sounds like a fine place
to begin.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.119
IUH: So net neutrality is the idea that internet
service providers should provide access to

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:27.060
all of the internet at the same speed: no
matter what content it is, no one gets any

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.060
preference.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.079
CCH: Fair definition; the question is: is
that a good idea?

00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:36.800
Like why should I, an internet service provider,
have to provide Game of Thrones via BitTorrent

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.490
at the same speed as I provide it through
HBO Go?

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.410
IUH: I don't know, cause you're not a law
enforcement agency and that's not your job,

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.120
but like, you shouldn't be determining how
your users use your service.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.910
It's the internet; you just provide access
to it and, but like, my concern is that if

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.670
you do it to BitTorrent that you'll do it
to, like, a legitimate competitor to your

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.090
business, like Netflix for example.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.329
CCH: No one is talking about doing that.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:01.870
IUH: I mean, you say that, but yeah they are.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:07.920
Like Comcast literally slowed down Netflix's
internet connection and forced them to pay

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.100
to get faster access to their customers; like
it happened already.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.210
CCH: Netflix is responsible for over 30% of
the bandwidth use of the US.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.270
Why should all of our customers, regardless
of whether they use Netflix, be required to

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.930
pay for the massive infrastructure upgrades
necessary to provide that service.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:25.749
Of course Netflix should pay.

00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:29.219
But, we're not talking about slowing down
Netflix to make them pay.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:32.240
We're just saying we're going to speed Netflix
up if they do pay.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.509
IUH: I mean if you sped Netflix up without
adding any new bandwidth, wouldn't you definitionally

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:40.009
slow someone else down in order to speed Netflix
up?

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:44.100
You are, right now, for the first time in
American history, making the internet a non-level

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.100
playing field.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:51.429
You talk high and mighty about economic efficiency,
but what creates great economies is not profit;

00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:53.689
it's competition, which is what you're trying
to destroy.

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:57.999
CCH: I mean, the internet's a mature industry
now; we can't keep running around like it's

00:01:57.999 --> 00:01:59.279
1998 hippie-fun-feel-good times.

00:01:59.279 --> 00:02:03.609
It's time to buckle down and turn this thing
into a real economic engine.

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:08.390
We want to charge customers for access to
content and charge content companies for access

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:09.640
to the customers.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.770
We get paid twice, it's great for us!

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.830
IUH: The internet is not your business model.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:22.170
The internet is a massive, technological,
and economic, and cultural force for good

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:28.280
that everyone should have equal access to,
and your place as a middle-man does not bequeath

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.730
you magical powers to extract value at every
turn!

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:37.040
You have no right to decide what information
goes at what speed through your pipes.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.760
CCH: I mean, don't we though?

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.150
We're already doing it with Netflix; we kind
of already set the precedent.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.670
Let's face it, this is a complicated legal
thing, and with the potential upside for us,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.280
we can afford a lot of lawyers and lobbyists.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.480
No offense, but I don't think you can play
on our level.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.210
IUH: I guess we'll see.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.270
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.270
Hi.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.270
I'm a little bit worried about this.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:04.950
I want the internet to remain open and neutral
in America... for selfish and also altruistic

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.950
reasons.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:06.950
You can help.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.760
And please do; the FCC is currently asking
for comments.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.630
Please go to comment at the FCC's website,
there's a link in the description.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.430
Tell them to classify broadband internet as
a common carrier service, which will prevent

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.220
telecoms from making the internet a closed,
non-neutral place.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.560
If you want to help in more long-term ways,
in the description there are a few organizations

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.880
that are working on these issues, actually
hiring lawyers and lobbyists to compete with

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:29.880
the telecoms.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.600
You can give them money; you can give them
your time; you can give them your action,

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.900
and you can call your congress people.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.600
I love the internet a lot and I bet that you
do too, and I am worried what's gonna happen

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.020
if we let the middlemen decide what the internet
is like.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.090
Frankly, I don't trust 'em.

